# Swiss German (Schwiizertüütsch) editor and language-server surfaces: the
# footer, the diagnostics panel, the variant picker, the accessibility button
# and the context-help panel. Selected by `uiLocale`.
#
# UNREVIEWED SEED. Machine-generated, pending review by a speaker (#1521).
# Correct anything here freely; nothing in it was written by a translator.
#
# **Orthography.** The Dieth-Schreibung, with double vowels for length
# («Siite», «wiiss», «Fääler») and **no ß anywhere**; see `chrome.ftl` for the
# full note. The missing ß is the sharpest visible break from `locales/de`.
#
# **This is a written standard over a spread of dialects**, and this file uses
# the Zurich-based koine rather than Bernese, Basel or Valais — the same trade
# `locales/sc` and `locales/rm` record. A deployment that wants a particular
# dialect supplies its own catalog as `localeResources`.
#
# **Do not edit this file toward Standard German**: «nöd» for «nicht», «chan»
# for «kann», «gseh» for «sehen», «Fähler» for «Fehler», «Siite» for «Seite»,
# «Chreis» for «Kreis» are the catalog, not typos.
#
# **There is no simple past** in Swiss German — the perfect does all of that
# work — which is why the past-tense sentences here read as perfects: «es sind
# kei Verstöss gfunde worde», never a preterite.
#
# **Number.** CLDR *does* have plural rules for `gsw` — `one` and `other` — so
# a category branch here is selected by Swiss German's own rules, unlike the
# seven catalogs of this batch CLDR has no data for. There is no `zero`, `two`,
# `few` or `many` category, so never write one; the branch is written wherever
# the noun actually changes («Verstoss» / «Verstöss»).
#
# **`WCAG`, `WCAG AA`, `DoenetML` and `XML` are names**, not words, and stay in
# English exactly as `locales/en` writes them. So do the DoenetML identifiers
# `styleNumber` and the attribute names in `help-reset-overrides`. Every
# symbolic selector — `$action`, `$status`, `$shortcut`, `$reason`,
# `$location`, `$allowed`, `$line`, `$perItem` — is kept byte for byte from
# English.


## The viewer's controls

editor-update-viewer =
    { $action ->
        [reset] Zrugsetze
       *[update] Aktualisiere
    }

# Swiss German puts the verb last, so the word arrives after the noun it acts
# on, the same shape `locales/de` uses.
editor-update-viewer-title =
    { $shortcut ->
        [none] D Aazeig { $word }
       *[other] D Aazeig { $word } { $shortcut }
    }


